@prefix dct: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-ic-precision> {
  ex:ic-df-map a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:rev-df ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-df-map-h, ex:ic-df-map-c ; cd:narration "Apply the preceding residual constraint to the examples that follow. The IC precision check has six readings and one fitted mean, leaving five residual df. The later t example deliberately uses a separate five-reading series, leaving four. The F example has two independent six-reading methods and needs ordered five and five df. Pearson uses five category counts constrained by a fixed total, leaving four independent count directions. The count depends on fitted constraints and the actual question; it is not a universal observations-minus-one rule. The five-residual warm-up illustrates the principle, not the sample size of every subsequent study."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-df-map-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Count degrees of freedom for the actual analytical study"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-df-map-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-df-map-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-df-map-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-df-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-process a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-process-h, ex:ic-process-v ; cd:narration "Begin with the analytical problem, not a density formula. A hypothetical ion-chromatography nitrate method previously had repeatability SD 0.10 mg/L. We suspect precision has deteriorated, perhaps because of an ageing separation column, but do not assume that cause. Measure one homogeneous water sample six times independently under repeatability conditions. We want to know whether the spread is unusually large relative to unchanged method precision, not whether the mean nitrate concentration is correct. Treat the validation SD as a fixed known reference for this teaching calculation. If it was estimated with material uncertainty, its estimation must enter a different justified variance-comparison procedure. The six results, deviations, variance, scaled statistic and decision on the following slides all belong to this one study."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-process-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "IC precision check: has nitrate repeatability deteriorated"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-process-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-process-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-process-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-process-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-data a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-data-h, ex:ic-data-c ; cd:narration "Here are the six constructed IC readings in order: 9.95, 10.15, 9.85, 10.25, 9.95 and 10.15 mg/L. Their fitted mean is 10.05 mg/L. Ask whether they look precise before calculating spread. Values close to ten do not establish trueness. The previous validation SD is 0.10 mg/L, treated as known. Assume independent normal errors with a common stable mean and variance; six symmetric results do not establish those assumptions. Prespecify a one-sided increased-spread alternative and 5% level. Now describe today's spread, then ask whether chance alone could explain it."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-data-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Could an unchanged IC method produce this much spread"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-data-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-data-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-data-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-deviations a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-deviations-h, ex:ic-deviations-t ; cd:narration "Use all six results, including duplicates. Deviations from fitted mean 10.05 mg/L are -0.10, +0.10, -0.20, +0.20, -0.10 and +0.10 mg/L. They sum to zero. Squaring removes sign and gives total 0.12 (mg/L) squared, not yet a variance. Divide by five to estimate variance; we will return to why fitting the mean leaves five independent residual directions after constructing the chi-square law."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-deviations-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "First describe today's spread; judge its rarity afterward"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-deviations-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-deviations-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-deviations-t a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-deviation-table ; cd:communicativeRole cd:TableRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasTableRow" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-spread-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-spread-h, ex:ic-spread-v, ex:ic-spread-c, ex:ic-spread-f ; cd:narration "This calculation answers only the descriptive part of our question. Squared sum 0.12 divided by five gives sample variance 0.024 (mg/L) squared and SD 0.154919 mg/L. We have not yet shown that the method deteriorated. The fixed reference SD is 0.10 mg/L, whose variance is 0.010 (mg/L) squared. An estimate above its reference can arise by chance, especially in a small series. We must ask how often an unchanged method would produce a variance at least 0.024, not simply note that 0.024 exceeds 0.010. Before any scaling or named reference law, look at repeated six-result series from that unchanged method. Their variance estimates will give the missing comparison."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-spread-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Today's spread is a description, not yet a test decision"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-spread-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-spread-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-spread-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-readings-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-spread-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-spread-context ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-spread-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-spread ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-squares a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-squares-h, ex:ic-squares-v, ex:ic-squares-c, ex:ic-squares-f ; cd:narration "Divide each deviation from mean 10.05 by reference SD 0.10 mg/L. For the 9.85 result, minus 0.20 divided by 0.10 is minus two; squaring gives four. The six scaled residuals are -1, 1, -2, 2, -1, 1. Their squared sum is twelve. Equivalently divide the original squared sum 0.12 by reference variance 0.01, or multiply sample variance 0.024 by five and divide by 0.01. Units cancel. The five-df normal-model reference calibrates this dimensionless statistic; the six centered residuals themselves are not independent standard normals."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-squares-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Scale, square and add: the observed statistic is 12"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-squares-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-squares-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-squares-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-residual-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-squares-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-q-context ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-squares-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-q ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-reference a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-reference-h, ex:ic-reference-v, ex:ic-reference-c, ex:ic-reference-f ; cd:narration "Assume for the reference calculation that true repeatability SD is still 0.10 mg/L. Six independent normal observations have six error coordinates. Fitting one common mean imposes a zero-sum residual constraint and removes one direction. An orthogonal change of coordinates leaves five independent standard-normal residual coordinates whose squared sum is the same scaled residual sum. This gives chi-square with five df. The original centered residuals are not individually independent standard normals, and counting six terms is not sufficient proof. Uppercase Q and S squared denote random quantities across new series; lowercase q twelve is the observed realization. The exact chi-square law holds at sigma equal to sigma0. For smaller true sigma the statistic is scaled down, which will make the boundary the least favorable case for the one-sided no-deterioration null."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-reference-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Six normal results, one fitted mean, five residual directions"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-reference-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-reference-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-reference-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-df-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-reference-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-law-context ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-reference-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-law ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-test a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-test-h, ex:ic-test-v, ex:ic-test-c, ex:ic-test-f ; cd:narration "State the predeclared hypotheses: true repeatability SD at most 0.10 mg/L versus larger SD. At the boundary, a chi-square-five statistic exceeds twelve with probability 0.0347878, about 3.48 percent. The orange region depicts that upper tail, not the probability the null is true. The upper five-percent critical value is 11.0704977, shown by the dashed line. Twelve exceeds it, so reject no deterioration at the chosen five-percent level under the assumptions. The boundary controls the composite null because a smaller true SD shifts Q down. This supplies evidence of poorer repeatability and supports investigating the analytical system under the laboratory's procedure. It does not diagnose an ageing column or itself authorize or prohibit release of real sample results. Bias, calibration and matrix effects are different questions. Reference SD estimation uncertainty, nonnormality or dependence would require a different justified calibration."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-test-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "12 exceeds 11.07: evidence of poorer IC repeatability"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-test-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-test-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-test-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-tail-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-test-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-decision-context ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-test-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-decision ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-repeated-series a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-repeated-h, ex:ic-repeated-c, ex:ic-repeated-v ; cd:narration "This is the missing link between observed spread and an inference about true precision. Imagine ten thousand independent six-result series, all generated with true SD 0.10 mg/L. Each fits its own mean and produces a different variance estimate. The saved deterministic simulation plots those unscaled sample variances, in squared concentration units. It is not a histogram of individual nitrate concentrations, and no change in true precision was simulated. Today's variance 0.024 must be located relative to this sampling variation. We could estimate its upper-tail frequency directly by simulation; in this realization 342 of ten thousand variances are at least 0.024, about 3.42 percent. A finite simulation is an approximation, not the exact law. To obtain the exact normal-model reference conveniently, next rescale this same variance information and derive the chi-square law. The scale change will preserve the event and its probability rather than create a second finding."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-repeated-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "What variance estimates would an unchanged method produce"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-repeated-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-repeated-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-repeated-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-variance-context ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-repeated-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-variance-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-t-transition a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:ic-t-transition-h, ex:ic-t-transition-v ; cd:narration "Close the six-result IC precision test and explicitly change studies. The following Student-t example returns to a separate constructed five-reading laboratory series: 9.6, 9.8, 10.0, 10.2 and 10.4 mg/L. Its mean is 10.0, sample variance 0.10 squared mg/L and SD about 0.31623 mg/L. It does not reuse the six IC values or their fixed reference SD. The question is uncertainty in the common analytical mean when true SD is unknown. Repeating normal five-result series gives normal sample means, but dividing their centered means by estimated rather than known SE gives t with four df. The process picture separates those denominator choices; the following slides compare them and report the mean interval for this distinct five-result dataset."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-t-transition-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Student-t: a separate five-result study of the analytical mean"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-t-transition-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:ic-t-transition-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:ic-t-transition-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
}
